How generative AI works — the full pipeline

At its core, GenAI is a next-token prediction engine. It doesn't "understand" language the way humans do — it's a massive statistical model that's learned to predict what word (or piece of a word) comes next, one step at a time, based on everything it's seen before.

Step 1: Tokenization

Before anything else, your input text gets broken into tokens — small chunks of text (words, sub-words, or characters). "Understanding" might become ["under", "stand", "ing"]. Models don't read raw text; they read sequences of token IDs (numbers).

Step 2: Embeddings

Each token ID gets converted into an embedding — a vector (list of numbers, often 1000+ dimensions) that captures its meaning in mathematical space. Words with similar meanings end up with similar vectors. This is how "king" and "queen" end up "close" to each other numerically.

Step 3: The Transformer (the engine room)

This is the core architecture behind almost all modern GenAI (GPT, Claude, Gemini). It's built from stacked layers, and the key mechanism inside is self-attention.

What you're seeing above: every token doesn't just look at the word right before it — it looks at every token in the sequence and asks "how relevant are you to me?" The thicker/brighter the line, the more that token influences the current one's meaning. This happens across many stacked layers, and each layer refines the representation further. This is called self-attention, and it's the mechanism that lets the model understand that in "the cat sat on the mat," "sat" is connected to "cat" (who's sitting) more than to "the."

Within each layer there are also multiple attention heads running in parallel — each one learning to focus on a different kind of relationship (grammar, meaning, position, etc.). That's called multi-head attention.

Step 4: Feedforward layers

After attention mixes information between tokens, each token passes through a feedforward network — a small neural net that transforms the representation further. Attention + feedforward = one transformer "block," and models stack dozens of these.

Step 5: Output — probability distribution

At the very end, the model produces a probability distribution over its entire vocabulary — basically a ranked guess of "what's the most likely next token?" For example, after "The sky is," it might assign 70% to "blue," 10% to "clear," 5% to "grey," etc.

Step 6: Sampling

The model then picks the next token — not always the highest-probability one (that would make output robotic and repetitive). Parameters like temperature control how random vs. deterministic this choice is. The chosen token gets added to the sequence, and the whole process repeats — this is why generation is described as autoregressive: each new token depends on all the ones before it.

The "theory" of the video is essentially: complexity and autonomy increase at each stage, and Agentic AI represents the current frontier — where AI stops being a tool you prompt and starts being a system that plans and executes on its own toward a goal.

