Transformer — explained simply

Think of the transformer like a group of friends reading a sentence together, where each friend represents one word.

Every friend (word) turns to every other friend in the sentence and asks: "How much do I need to pay attention to you to understand my own meaning here?"

Example: "The cat sat on the mat because it was tired."

The word "it" turns to every other word and checks relevance:

"it" → "cat" → very relevant (this is probably what "it" refers to)
"it" → "mat" → barely relevant
"it" → "tired" → somewhat relevant

This checking-in process is called attention — it's just the model figuring out which words matter most to understanding each other word.

Now here's the "transformer" part: this attention step happens, then each word's understanding gets refined a little (a small processing step called a feedforward layer), and then the whole thing repeats — dozens of times, stacked on top of each other like floors in a building. Each floor (layer) makes the model's understanding of the sentence a little richer and more nuanced.

By the time the sentence has passed through all the floors, the model has a very deep, contextual understanding of every word — not just what it means alone, but what it means in that specific sentence.

In one sentence: A transformer is a stack of layers where every word repeatedly checks in with every other word to build up a rich understanding of the whole sentence.

Probability distribution — explained simply

Once the model understands the sentence, it has to guess: "What word comes next?"

It doesn't just pick one word confidently — it gives a score to every possible word in its vocabulary (tens of thousands of words), like a ranked guess.

Example — after "I love drinking my morning...":

Word	Probability
coffee	68%
tea	20%
juice	6%
smoothie	4%
shoe	0.001%
Next word prediction after "I love drinking my morning..."
Probability (%)

This whole ranked list is the probability distribution — every possible next word gets a percentage chance, and they all add up to 100%.

The model then picks one — usually leaning toward the higher-probability options, but not always the very top one (a setting called temperature controls how "safe" vs "creative" this pick is). That chosen word gets added to the sentence, and the entire process — attention, layers, probability distribution — runs again to pick the next word. And again. And again — one word at a time, until the response is complete.

In one sentence: The probability distribution is just the model's ranked list of "here's how likely each possible next word is," and it picks from that list to keep writing.
